WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.400 --> 00:00:03.920
A memorable last scene can&nbsp;
make a good movie great.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:03.920 --> 00:00:09.440
But which Marvel movie really broke the mold?&nbsp;
What exactly was Nic Cage doing in The Wicker&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:09.440 --> 00:00:16.160
Man? How did one British comedy inspire a&nbsp;
thousand funerals? Keep watching to find out.

00:00:16.160 --> 00:00:19.120
Warning: Spoilers ahead for&nbsp;
all the movies discussed.

00:00:20.320 --> 00:00:26.720
Fight Club, David Fincher's 1999 adaptation&nbsp;
of the 1996 Chuck Palahniuk novel,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:26.720 --> 00:00:30.560
is one of those movies where the big&nbsp;
twist ending forces you to reassess&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:30.560 --> 00:00:36.560
everything you've just seen. The reveal that Tyler&nbsp;
Durden is the Narrator's alternate personality&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:36.560 --> 00:00:41.840
might raise a little confusion about specific plot&nbsp;
mechanics, but it immediately brings the story's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:41.840 --> 00:00:47.040
themes into focus. Tyler is the unhealthy&nbsp;
projection of the Narrator's own rage,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:47.040 --> 00:00:53.040
self-loathing, and toxic masculinity, an&nbsp;
internal antagonist who needs to be defeated.

00:00:53.040 --> 00:00:58.560
The climactic twist comes straight from the book,&nbsp;
but the movie offers its own spin on the ultimate&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:58.560 --> 00:01:04.400
resolution. Though the Narrator kills Tyler,&nbsp;
Project Mayhem actually succeeds, and Tyler's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:04.400 --> 00:01:10.880
carefully planted bombs are detonated. In the&nbsp;
darkly hilarious finale, the Narrator tells Marla:

00:01:10.880 --> 00:01:13.120
"You met me at a very strange time in my life."

00:01:13.920 --> 00:01:17.520
Buildings explode around them as&nbsp;
The Pixies' "Where Is My Mind"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:17.520 --> 00:01:21.600
leads into the credits. This ending&nbsp;
was actually censored in China,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:21.600 --> 00:01:25.840
although, ironically, the censored&nbsp;
version is closer to Palahniuk's novel.

00:01:26.560 --> 00:01:31.520
In many ways, Doctor Strange is one of&nbsp;
the more formulaic Marvel origin stories,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:31.520 --> 00:01:35.920
with Stephen Strange's character arc having a lot&nbsp;
in common with that of Tony Stark's in the first&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:35.920 --> 00:01:40.000
Iron Man movie. What a relief it is,&nbsp;
then, when the climax of the movie&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:40.000 --> 00:01:45.280
breaks significantly from the standard MCU&nbsp;
formula. Whereas most Marvel movies build&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:45.280 --> 00:01:51.200
up to giant fight scenes between the heroes and&nbsp;
villains, Scott Derrickson's 2016 film finds a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:51.200 --> 00:01:57.040
more clever method for the Sorcerer Supreme to&nbsp;
save Earth from the cosmic terror of Dormammu.

00:01:57.040 --> 00:02:01.920
Strange's strategy initially appears to be&nbsp;
an attempt at diplomacy — he announces his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:01.920 --> 00:02:08.320
intention to "bargain" with Dormammu, at which&nbsp;
point the Dark Dimension ruler promptly kills him.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:08.320 --> 00:02:14.320
It soon becomes clear, however, that Strange's&nbsp;
true plan is to annoy Dormammu into submission,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:14.320 --> 00:02:17.600
as he has trapped both himself&nbsp;
and Dormammu in a time loop in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:17.600 --> 00:02:21.840
which Strange keeps coming back to&nbsp;
be killed again and again and again.

00:02:22.840 --> 00:02:27.440
"Dormammu, I've come to bargain."
Eventually, Dormammu gets so sick&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:27.440 --> 00:02:32.400
of the loop that he agrees to Strange's terms&nbsp;
to leave Earth alone. No doubt, more superhero&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:32.400 --> 00:02:37.360
movies could benefit from the inventive use of&nbsp;
superpowers displayed in this unique showdown.

00:02:38.400 --> 00:02:43.440
Director Adam McKay's Don't Look Up was&nbsp;
one of the most divisive films of 2021.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:43.440 --> 00:02:48.560
It was one of Netflix's most watched movies&nbsp;
ever and accrued many award nominations,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:48.560 --> 00:02:52.800
including one for Best Picture at&nbsp;
the Oscars, but reviews were mixed.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:52.800 --> 00:02:58.240
Even those who generally disliked the apocalyptic&nbsp;
satire, however, have acknowledged that the film&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:58.240 --> 00:03:02.720
is at its best when it finds genuine pathos&nbsp;
in the characters' final night on Earth.

00:03:02.720 --> 00:03:08.240
With the ultra-rich escaping to outer space and&nbsp;
the comet about to deliver the extinction of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:08.240 --> 00:03:14.240
mankind, Dr. Randall Mindy decides to spend one&nbsp;
last dinner together with friends and family.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:14.240 --> 00:03:18.320
Yule, the Christian anarchist&nbsp;
skater played by Timothée Chalamet,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:18.320 --> 00:03:23.200
leads the group in prayer before dinner, and&nbsp;
everyone expresses the things they're grateful for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:23.200 --> 00:03:26.960
even as the world is literally ending&nbsp;
around them. Critics may have disagreed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:26.960 --> 00:03:31.840
about the movie as a whole, but there's&nbsp;
no denying the power of its final moments.

00:03:32.080 --> 00:03:36.160
Many of Pixar's movies save their&nbsp;
most emotional moments for the end.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:36.160 --> 00:03:40.880
The ending of Monsters University,&nbsp;
the 2013 prequel to Monsters Inc.,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:40.880 --> 00:03:45.680
stands out by elevating what is otherwise&nbsp;
a cute but unexceptional parody of college&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:45.680 --> 00:03:51.040
movies into something that genuinely lives&nbsp;
up to Pixar's high standard of quality.

00:03:51.040 --> 00:03:54.080
There are many family movies&nbsp;
about following your dreams,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:54.080 --> 00:03:59.280
but Monsters University is one of the few to&nbsp;
acknowledge that dreams can fail and change. No&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:59.280 --> 00:04:05.760
matter how hard he tries, Mike Wazowski realizes&nbsp;
he's never going to be scary. That's okay, though,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:05.760 --> 00:04:10.320
because he finds there are other things he's&nbsp;
good at — such as working as a scare assistant.

00:04:10.320 --> 00:04:15.760
"Mike, you're not scary. Not even&nbsp;
a little. But you are fearless."

00:04:15.760 --> 00:04:20.800
Both Mike and Sully end up expelled for all&nbsp;
the trouble they cause, but they find they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:20.800 --> 00:04:26.080
don't need to follow the set path of academia&nbsp;
to succeed. Instead, they apply for jobs in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:26.080 --> 00:04:30.560
mailroom at Monsters Inc., and the credits&nbsp;
sequence shows them gradually working their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:30.560 --> 00:04:35.760
way up the corporate ladder. It's an unusual but&nbsp;
positive message of flexibility and compromise.

00:04:36.800 --> 00:04:42.400
Writer-director Céline Sciamma is talented at many&nbsp;
things, but her greatest talent might be found in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:42.400 --> 00:04:48.880
the endings to her movies. Portrait of a Lady on&nbsp;
Fire, her 2019 historical lesbian romance, is a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:48.880 --> 00:04:54.480
slow burn with a powerful conclusion. Romantic&nbsp;
tensions gradually build up between the painter&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:54.480 --> 00:05:01.040
Marianne and her aristocratic subject Héloïse,&nbsp;
but their affair is brief, and Marianne only sees&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:01.040 --> 00:05:06.480
Héloïse twice after it's over. The first time&nbsp;
is at an art exhibit; the second time is at the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:06.480 --> 00:05:12.160
orchestra, and it's this ending which turns the&nbsp;
film into one of the all-time great tearjerkers.

00:05:12.160 --> 00:05:17.600
Portrait of a Lady on Fire uses music extremely&nbsp;
sparingly — only in scenes where the characters&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:17.600 --> 00:05:22.240
are actually performing or singing. As a result,&nbsp;
when music is heard in the film, its effect is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:22.240 --> 00:05:27.680
extremely powerful. The orchestra in the final&nbsp;
scene is playing the Presto from "Summer" in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:27.680 --> 00:05:32.480
Vivaldi's [vih-VAHL-dee] Four Seasons, the same&nbsp;
piece of music Marianne once played for Héloïse.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:32.480 --> 00:05:37.360
Héloïse doesn't know Marianne is observing her&nbsp;
in the audience for this concert, but when she&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:37.360 --> 00:05:42.080
hears this music, she appears overwhelmed&nbsp;
with the memories of their love. It's an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:42.080 --> 00:05:47.520
incredible piece of filmmaking on Sciamma's part,&nbsp;
supported by a superb performance by Adèle Haenel.

00:05:48.400 --> 00:05:53.520
At the start of 1973's F for Fake,&nbsp;
filmmaker Orson Welles assures the audience:

00:05:54.080 --> 00:05:59.600
"During the next hour, everything you hear from&nbsp;
us is really true, and based on solid fact."

00:06:00.560 --> 00:06:05.840
He spends the next hour talking about various&nbsp;
real life hoaxes, from art forgeries to the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:05.840 --> 00:06:10.720
fictional news broadcasts in his own&nbsp;
1938 War of the Worlds radio drama.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:10.720 --> 00:06:14.720
But F for Fake runs longer than an&nbsp;
hour, and in its final 28 minutes,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:14.720 --> 00:06:18.800
it becomes one of the very "fakes"&nbsp;
the first hour was documenting.

00:06:18.800 --> 00:06:23.040
Welles is honest about his fakery, of&nbsp;
course. All his assurances of truth at&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:23.040 --> 00:06:28.000
the beginning were clearly stated to last&nbsp;
just one hour, and at the end of the film,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:28.000 --> 00:06:32.080
he openly admits to "lying [his] head&nbsp;
off" during the 17-minute story about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:32.080 --> 00:06:37.520
his partner and co-writer Oja Kodar modeling&nbsp;
for and swindling Picasso. In an era of "fake&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:37.520 --> 00:06:42.720
news," Welles' trolling deconstruction of the&nbsp;
documentary format is more relevant than ever.

00:06:44.000 --> 00:06:48.160
Christopher Nolan's 2006 film The&nbsp;
Prestige concludes with several&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:48.160 --> 00:06:54.800
mind-blowing twists. Each of the magicians is&nbsp;
hiding secrets: For Hugh Jackman's Robert Angier,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:54.800 --> 00:06:59.280
the secret is that he's able to pull off&nbsp;
the "Transported Man" magic trick through&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:59.280 --> 00:07:04.000
actual magic of sorts, cloning himself&nbsp;
with a machine built by Nikola Tesla.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:04.000 --> 00:07:08.400
Christian Bale's Alfred Borden performs&nbsp;
the trick using a very different secret:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:08.400 --> 00:07:12.720
He's not actually one person, but twin&nbsp;
brothers sharing a single identity.

00:07:12.720 --> 00:07:16.160
One Borden is hanged after&nbsp;
being framed for killing Angier,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:16.160 --> 00:07:21.760
who's secretly still alive under the identity of&nbsp;
Lord Caldlow. The other Borden gets his revenge&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:21.760 --> 00:07:27.600
and kills the last remaining Angier, whose theater&nbsp;
burns down with all his dead clones inside.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:27.600 --> 00:07:32.000
Great twist endings are hard to pull off,&nbsp;
but The Prestige is a movie designed around&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:32.000 --> 00:07:36.880
its many revelations, meticulously following&nbsp;
the steps that make a great magic trick work.

00:07:37.680 --> 00:07:42.400
Set in a motel outside of Disney World,&nbsp;
The Florida Project centers around Moonee,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:42.400 --> 00:07:47.600
a six-year-old girl having fun with her friends&nbsp;
while her loving but neglectful single mother,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:47.600 --> 00:07:51.440
Halley, struggles to make ends meet.&nbsp;
It's a movie that's repetitive by design&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:51.440 --> 00:07:54.560
to best get across the reality of life in poverty,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:54.560 --> 00:07:59.440
but the ending shakes up this repetition in a&nbsp;
manner both heartbreaking and absolutely stunning.

00:08:00.080 --> 00:08:05.040
Eventually, things get bad enough that child&nbsp;
services come to take Moonee away from Halley.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:05.040 --> 00:08:08.880
In this moment of deep pain, Moonee&nbsp;
and her friend Jancey escape and run&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:08.880 --> 00:08:13.360
away to the Magic Kingdom. People have&nbsp;
debated whether the Disney World scene&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:13.360 --> 00:08:17.520
is meant to be real or the children's&nbsp;
fantasy — but however you interpret it,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:17.520 --> 00:08:21.360
the conclusion makes the film as a&nbsp;
whole even more memorable and affecting.

00:08:22.480 --> 00:08:27.920
Fans might argue over which Monty Python movie&nbsp;
is the funniest, but Monty Python's Life of Brian&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:27.920 --> 00:08:33.440
is definitely the one with the strongest story —&nbsp;
one which leads to the strongest ending by far.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:33.440 --> 00:08:39.360
This irreverent, often-censored 1979 comedy&nbsp;
follows Brian, a "very naughty boy" living&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:39.360 --> 00:08:44.400
in proximity to Jesus who is accidentally&nbsp;
mistaken for the Messiah. At the end of the film,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:44.400 --> 00:08:50.160
Brian has been sentenced to death by crucifixion,&nbsp;
and all attempts to save him go horribly awry.

00:08:50.160 --> 00:08:51.680
"Rej, what are you going to—"

00:08:51.680 --> 00:08:53.840
"Goodbye, Brian. And thanks."

00:08:54.480 --> 00:08:59.200
Unable to do anything to escape his slow and&nbsp;
painful death, the other crucified men cheer&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:59.200 --> 00:09:05.600
Brian up by singing "Always Look on the Bright&nbsp;
Side of Life." Optimistic, nihilistic, absurdist,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:05.600 --> 00:09:11.680
extremely British — there are lots of ways you can&nbsp;
describe the comedic juxtaposition of Eric Idle's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:11.680 --> 00:09:16.160
silly, upbeat song with the direness of&nbsp;
its context. Whatever you want to call it,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:16.160 --> 00:09:20.880
though, it clearly struck a chord. In 2014,&nbsp;
"Always Look on the Bright Side of Life"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:20.880 --> 00:09:25.360
was revealed to have been the most frequently&nbsp;
requested funeral song in the United Kingdom.

00:09:26.320 --> 00:09:32.000
Neil LaBute's 2006 remake of The Wicker Man is not&nbsp;
considered a good horror movie by most people's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:32.000 --> 00:09:37.440
standards. In the final moments of its unrated&nbsp;
cut, however, it certainly becomes memorable.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:37.440 --> 00:09:42.800
Though there are several amusing displays of&nbsp;
overacting from Nicolas Cage throughout the film,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:42.800 --> 00:09:46.800
none compare to the scene where the&nbsp;
villagers torture him with bees,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:46.800 --> 00:09:49.440
resulting in the now-legendary line:

00:09:49.440 --> 00:09:53.840
"Not the bees! Oh, they're in my eyes, my eyes!"

00:09:55.840 --> 00:10:01.600
In a 2022 interview with IndieWire, Cage claimed&nbsp;
the comedy of this scene was intentional,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:01.600 --> 00:10:07.360
and that he and LaBute actually wanted the film&nbsp;
to go further with the ridiculousness. He said:

00:10:07.360 --> 00:10:12.880
"It probably would've been more clear how funny&nbsp;
it was if [producer] Avi Lerner let me have the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:12.880 --> 00:10:18.400
handlebar mustache that I wanted to wear and be&nbsp;
burned in the bear suit. That would've been so&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:18.400 --> 00:10:22.960
horrifying, but they didn't go for that because&nbsp;
all the comedy would've emerged from this horror."

00:10:22.960 --> 00:10:27.680
He pointed to the horrifying yet also&nbsp;
darkly comedic conclusion of Midsommar&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:27.680 --> 00:10:31.840
as a more successful example of what&nbsp;
The Wicker Man remake was aiming for.

00:10:32.320 --> 00:10:38.320
Michael Curtiz's 1942 drama Casablanca is a&nbsp;
movie so beloved as a classic that basically&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:38.320 --> 00:10:43.040
every line has become a cliché — and yet&nbsp;
it's still a delight to watch 80 years later.

00:10:43.040 --> 00:10:47.360
From beginning to end, Casablanca&nbsp;
is a brilliantly entertaining movie,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:47.360 --> 00:10:52.080
with clever writing and captivating romantic&nbsp;
chemistry between Humphrey Bogart's Rick Blane&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:52.080 --> 00:10:56.080
and Ingrid Bergman's Ilsa Lund.&nbsp;
It's the story's conclusion,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:56.080 --> 00:11:00.640
however, that truly seals this as one of&nbsp;
the greatest American films ever made.

00:11:00.640 --> 00:11:05.680
Rick, a character who was once a celebrated&nbsp;
freedom fighter but has since become cynical&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:05.680 --> 00:11:11.520
and disillusioned, regains his heroism at a time&nbsp;
when the world truly needs it. He sacrifices his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:11.520 --> 00:11:16.240
change to stay with Ilsa in order to help&nbsp;
her and her husband escape from the Nazis.

00:11:16.240 --> 00:11:17.360
"Here's looking at you, kid."

00:11:18.320 --> 00:11:23.120
Indeed, Casablanca is a work of wartime&nbsp;
propaganda made with such moral heft&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:23.120 --> 00:11:27.840
and great storytelling that it elevates&nbsp;
itself to become a powerful work of art.

00:11:27.920 --> 00:11:33.600
George Romero's Night of the Living Dead shocked&nbsp;
moviegoers in 1968 with its groundbreaking gore&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:33.600 --> 00:11:38.720
and fresh take on the zombie mythos. For all&nbsp;
the terror of its flesh-eating undead, though,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:38.720 --> 00:11:43.920
the ending of the film shows how ordinary&nbsp;
humans can be the scariest monsters of all. Ben,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:43.920 --> 00:11:48.880
the movie's Black hero, has done his best to&nbsp;
survive throughout the film, but in the end,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:48.880 --> 00:11:53.440
he's killed by some white men with guns&nbsp;
who seemingly mistake him for a zombie.

00:11:53.440 --> 00:11:58.400
Romero didn't intentionally write Night of the&nbsp;
Living Dead with race in mind, but regardless of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:58.400 --> 00:12:03.920
what the filmmaker initially intended, the ending&nbsp;
has widely been viewed as a subversive commentary&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:03.920 --> 00:12:09.920
on racism. Its implications are, unfortunately, no&nbsp;
less scary today than they were when the movie was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:09.920 --> 00:12:15.280
first released. You can credit Night of the Living&nbsp;
Dead, and particularly its ending, for helping&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:15.280 --> 00:12:20.400
lead the way for such racially-conscious&nbsp;
horror films as Candyman and Get Out.

